Add the FocalTech FT9201 (2808:93a9) driver to libfprint's build.

Two one-line additions register the driver the same way every other
built-in libfprint driver is registered:

  * meson.build          - an entry in the "drivers_info" dictionary.
    An empty options dict is correct here: the driver is not optional,
    not virtual, needs no helper library, is not SPI, and is endian
    independent (endian dependence is opt-in via "endian_dependent").
  * libfprint/meson.build - an entry in the "driver_sources" dictionary.

From these, libfprint's build generates fpi-drivers.c, which calls
fpi_device_focaltech_ft9201_get_type () - the symbol the driver's
G_DECLARE_FINAL_TYPE declares.

Both entries sit next to "vcom5s" on purpose. That is a dormant part of
both dictionaries, so the patch context stays stable across libfprint
updates. Do not move them next to "focaltech_moc": Fedora backports new
Focal reader IDs into that region, which is exactly where a future
rebase would conflict.

Applies to libfprint v1.94.100.
---
diff -u a/libfprint/meson.build b/libfprint/meson.build
--- a/libfprint/meson.build
+++ b/libfprint/meson.build
@@ -103,6 +103,7 @@
     'aes3500' : files('drivers/aes3500.c'),
     'aes4000' : files('drivers/aes4000.c'),
     'vcom5s' : files('drivers/vcom5s.c'),
+    'focaltech_ft9201' : files('drivers/focaltech_ft9201.c'),
     'vfs101' : files('drivers/vfs101.c'),
     'vfs301' : files(
         'drivers/vfs301.c',
diff -u a/meson.build b/meson.build
--- a/meson.build
+++ b/meson.build
@@ -133,6 +133,7 @@
     'egismoc': {},
     'egis_etu905': {},
     'vcom5s': {},
+    'focaltech_ft9201': {},
     'synaptics': {},
     'elan': { 'endian_dependent': true },
     'elanmoc': {},
